[[community-detection]]
Community detection

Community detection groups the nodes of a graph into clusters that are more densely
connected to each other than to the rest of the graph. Applied to a call graph, it
surfaces the natural feature boundaries of a codebase — the sets of functions that
work together — without relying on the directory layout, which often reflects history
rather than cohesion.

The Louvain method optimizes a quantity called modularity by repeatedly moving nodes
into the neighbouring community that most improves it, then collapsing each community
into a super-node and repeating. The Leiden refinement adds a guarantee that every
detected community is internally well connected, fixing a known failure mode where
Louvain can occasionally produce a cluster that is internally split. Either way the
clusters make better routing targets than a single flat project hub.
